.TH C-XPRA 1 "10 November 2026" "c-xpra 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
c-xpra \- minimal Xpra client written in C
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B c-xpra
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-\-password\-file=\c
.IR file ]
.I target
.br
.B c-xpra
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B c-xpra
connects to an Xpra server and shows the windows of a seamless session on the
local X11 display with their icons and the application cursors, forwarding pointer and keyboard input to the server
and ringing the local bell when the session rings its own.
Server lifecycle events and notifications are printed on standard output.
.PP
It implements only what is needed for that: there is no audio, clipboard,
system tray, file transfer or printing support, and
pictures are only received as raw pixels, optionally LZ4 compressed.
.PP
The server must be Xpra version 8.0 or later.
.SH TARGETS
.TP
.BR tcp:// \fIhost\fR[ : \fIport\fR]
A plain TCP connection.
The default port is 14500.
IPv6 addresses are written in brackets:
.BR tcp://[::1]:14500 .
.TP
.IR host : port
The same as
.BR tcp:// .
.TP
.BI : display
A local session, using the socket
.BI $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xpra/ display /socket
or, for older servers,
.BI $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/xpra/ hostname - display
and
.BI ~/.xpra/ hostname - display .
.TP
.BI socket: path ", " socket:// path ", " path
A Unix domain socket.
.PP
TLS, SSH and WebSocket connections are not supported directly: use an
.BR ssh (1)
tunnel to reach a remote server securely.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Log the type of every packet sent and received.
.TP
.BI \-\-password\-file= file
Read the password from the first line of
.IR file .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print command usage and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XPRA_PASSWORD
The password to use when the server requests authentication, unless
.B \-\-password\-file
is given.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X11 display to show the windows on.
.TP
.B USER
The user name sent to the server.
.TP
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ", " HOME
Used to locate the socket of a local session.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Only the
.B hmac+sha256
challenge / response is supported, so the password itself is never sent to the
server.
When no password is supplied and the server requests one, it is read from the
terminal with echo disabled.
.PP
The Xpra protocol does not authenticate the server or encrypt the session:
use an
.BR ssh (1)
tunnel on networks you do not trust.
.SH EXAMPLES
Connect to local display 10:
.PP
.RS
.B c-xpra :10
.RE
.PP
Connect to a server on the default TCP port, with a password:
.PP
.RS
.B XPRA_PASSWORD=secret c-xpra tcp://server.example.com
.RE
.PP
Connect to a remote session through an SSH tunnel:
.PP
.RS
.B ssh \-N \-L 14500:127.0.0.1:14500 server.example.com &
.br
.B c-xpra tcp://127.0.0.1:14500
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B c-xpra
exits with status 0 when the user closes the connection with
.B SIGINT
or
.BR SIGTERM ,
or when the server closes it for a normal reason (ie: the session was
detached or shut down), and 1 on errors, including invalid command-line usage,
connection failures and authentication failures.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xpra (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
Project documentation:
.B https://github.com/Xpra-org/c-xpra
